CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: HC1
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Scope check: verified against .swarm/HC-RUN-SPEC.md acceptance criteria 1-6,
working in a `cp -a` copy at
/tmp/claude-1000/.../scratchpad/hc1check (never ran the tier3 oracle or its
port 18093; used port 18096 for my own probe server; never touched
.swarm/tier3/HC1/).

CONFIRMED CORRECT (attacks that did NOT break it):
- HealthcareCoverageStart / ClippedHealthcareMonths are the single exported
  derivation+clipping functions; grep confirms every non-test multiplication
  of a healthcare target by a month count in metrics.go and handlers.go
  routes through ClippedHealthcareMonths.
- All non-test call sites (handlers.go x3, metrics.go's Comparison,
  mcpsvc/spend/summary.go) derive coverage start from `data.Active()` /
  `ts.Active()` — the full, unfiltered, post-duplicate-resolution set —
  before any date-range filter. Verified by grep and by reading each site.
- Comparison() derives coverage start once from the full set and passes the
  same (coverageStart, hasCoverage) into both the current-window and
  compare-window Calculate() calls — no per-window leak/re-derivation.
- verdict.go's rendered-string-sum fix (Healthcare.Delta's cents derived as
  Delta's cents minus Living's cents) is present and there's a dedicated
  fractional-cent regression test (verdict_fractional_cent_test.go).
- Live probe against a "zero Health Insurance transactions, healthcare
  target=1000/mo configured" fixture (my own scratch data dir, server on
  127.0.0.1:18096): dashboard /dashboard/kpis correctly rendered "$0.00" /
  "No premium target set" for the Healthcare card, omitted the Health line
  from the Budget-card breakdown entirely, and showed no NaN/Inf anywhere.
  This matches spec's "no coverage" edge case exactly.
- go build ./... and go test ./... pass (per manifest tail); confirmed the
  metrics package's own new tests (TestHealthcareCoverageStart_*,
  TestClippedHealthcareMonths_*, TestCalculateMetrics_Coverage*) cover
  earliest-bill selection, refunds-ignored, and all four Calculate-level
  edge cases (before/inside/after window, no-coverage-flag, exact boundary).

DEFECT 1 (concrete, reproducible, in-scope) — cross-surface disagreement in
summarize_spending on the exact "no coverage" edge case the spec calls out:

Built the same probe fixture above (transactions.csv with income + 6 months
of groceries, ZERO Health Insurance rows; settings/whatif.json has
monthly_healthcare=1000, monthly_living_expenses=1000) and hit both surfaces
for the same window 2026-01-01..2026-06-30:

  $ curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:18096/dashboard/kpis?start=2026-01-01&end=2026-06-30"
  -> Monthly Healthcare card: "$0.00", "No premium target set", no Health
     line in the Budget breakdown (correctly suppressed).

  $ curl -s -X POST http://127.0.0.1:18096/mcp ... summarize_spending
    start_date=2026-01-01 end_date=2026-06-30
  -> "budget":{"combined_cumulative_delta":1253.39,
       "healthcare_monthly_actual":0,"healthcare_monthly_delta":-1000,
       "healthcare_monthly_target":1000,"living_monthly_actual":1210.77,
       "living_monthly_delta":210.77,"living_monthly_target":1000,
       "months_in_range":5.95}

summarize_spending reports healthcare_monthly_target=1000 and
healthcare_monthly_delta=-1000 (i.e. "$1000/mo under a real healthcare
budget") for a period where there is NO coverage at all — the exact phantom
figure HC-RUN-SPEC.md's Problem section describes and HC1 exists to kill,
now surfaced through the MCP tool instead of the dashboard. The dashboard
correctly reports "no target"; summarize_spending contradicts it on the
identical window and ledger. This directly violates acceptance criterion 4
("... and summarize_spending all reflect clipped accrual and agree with
each other") and the edge-case text ("cards/verdict omit the healthcare
budget lines exactly as they do today when no target is configured" — this
was not extended to summary.go's per-field output).

Root cause: internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/summary.go's budgetView
population gate is `if m.HasBudgetTarget || m.HasHealthcareTarget` (line
~311, untouched by this diff per `git diff master -- summary.go`), but
inside that block it unconditionally copies m.HealthcareTarget /
HealthcareActual / HealthcarePerMonthDelta regardless of
m.HasHealthcareTarget. Pre-HC1 this was harmless because HasHealthcareTarget
was true iff healthcareTarget>0, so whenever it was false the raw fields
were already 0. HC1 changed HasHealthcareTarget's meaning to also require
coverage, so now HasHealthcareTarget can be false while HealthcareTarget is
still a positive configured value — and summary.go never zeroes/gates on
that, unlike kpis.html/verdict.go which do gate on it. This is the
split-classification defect class named in the run's own recurring-defects
list, just found on the field-suppression axis rather than the
month-multiplication axis.
Confirms via `git diff master -- internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/summary_test.go`
that this file was NOT touched by the HC1 diff at all — no test exists for
the (healthTarget configured, hasCoverage=false) combination, so nothing
caught it.

DEFECT 2 (acceptance-criterion gap, test coverage) — criterion 3 requires
unit tests covering "duplicates excluded" for the coverage-start
derivation; none exist:

  $ grep -in "duplicate" internal/services/metrics/metrics_test.go   -> no output
  $ grep -rln "HealthcareCoverageStart" --include=*_test.go .        -> only metrics_test.go
  $ grep -rn "Suppressed:" --include=*_test.go internal/handlers/dashboard internal/services/mcpsvc
      -> only pre-existing, unrelated (Netflix) fixtures

I wrote a throwaway probe test to check whether the underlying MECHANISM is
actually sound (it is — HealthcareCoverageStart itself does not filter
Suppressed transactions; every real call site correctly pre-filters via
.Active() before calling it, confirmed by grep across all non-test call
sites). So this is a real gap against the written acceptance criterion (no
test exercises "duplicates excluded"), not a behavioral bug in the shipped
code path — reported for completeness but Defect 1 is the substantive
reason for the FAIL.

Backlog observations (out of scope, not grounds for FAIL):
- The Monthly Healthcare sparkline's `data-target` attribute in kpis.html
  still renders a flat `HealthcareTarget` reference line across ALL trend
  months, including any month before coverage started (charts.js
  renderSparkline draws one constant target line, it cannot vary per
  point). This is unchanged by HC1 (HealthcareTarget itself is documented
  to remain the flat un-clipped monthly rate; only totals/cumulative
  figures are clipped) and is not in the task's enumerated consumer list —
  flagging for the backlog, not failing on it.
- handleKPIDetail (/dashboard/kpi/{kpiType}) has no "healthcare"/"budget"
  kpiType branch at all, so it is not a healthcare-target consumer and is
  correctly untouched.
